.TH QFMT 1 "2026 April 02" "Qore 2.3" "Qore Programming Language"
.SH NAME
qfmt \- canonical code formatter for Qore
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qfmt
[\fIoptions\fP]
[\fIfile\fP]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBqfmt\fR formats Qore source code according to the canonical Qore coding
style.
It supports formatting in-place, showing a unified diff, or checking whether
files are already formatted (for CI pipelines).
.PP
When no files are given and stdin is not a terminal, \fBqfmt\fR reads from
stdin and writes the formatted output to stdout.
.PP
The following file extensions are processed:
\fB.qc\fP, \fB.qm\fP, \fB.qore\fP, \fB.qr\fP, \fB.qsd\fP, \fB.qfd\fP,
\fB.qrd\fP, \fB.qtest\fP.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-in\-place
Format files in-place (overwrite the originals).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-diff
Show a unified diff of the changes instead of the formatted output.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-check
Exit with status 0 if all files are already formatted, or 1 if changes
are needed.
Useful for CI checks.
.TP
.B \-\-stdin
Read from stdin (this is the default when no files are given and stdin
is not a terminal).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help text and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All files are already formatted (with \fB\-c\fP), or formatting succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
One or more files need formatting (with \fB\-c\fP), or an error occurred.
.SH EXAMPLES
Format a file in-place:
.RS
.B qfmt \-i mymodule.qm
.RE
.PP
Show what would change:
.RS
.B qfmt \-d qlib/MyModule/*.qc
.RE
.PP
Check formatting in CI:
.RS
.B qfmt \-c qlib/
.RE
.PP
Format stdin:
.RS
.B cat myfile.q | qfmt
.RE
